.TH INTRO 4
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to file servers
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I "file server"
is a program that answers 9P2000.L on a pipe and gives a tree of files to
whatever mounts it.
This section describes each one: what it serves, where it is
conventionally mounted, and how it stops.
.PP
A server is started like any program.
It makes a pipe, posts one end under a name in
.B /srv
(see
.IR srv (3)),
and its parent exits once the name is there, so a shell that started it
can go on to mount it on the next line.
A client reaches the tree with
.IR mount ,
which walks the posted pipe and grafts the server's root onto a name in
the caller's own name space; see
.IR bind (1)
and
.IR bind (2).
Most servers here stop on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the
posted name are both gone; a few take a
.B Tremove
on a file of their own as their stop instead, and each page says which.
.PP
.IR init (8)
starts
.IR kbdfs (4)
and
.IR intuition (4)
once the disks are mounted, so a keyboard and a window are the first
things a booted machine has beyond the console.
.PP
The servers:
.TP 10
.IR ramfs (4)
a teaching server: two fixed files, in a program's own segments
.TP
.IR memfs (4)
a file tree of any shape, kept on the heap
.TP
.IR fatfs (4)
the FAT volume the machine booted from
.TP
.IR kfs (4)
the disk of Vectra's own, where home directories live
.TP
.IR kbdfs (4)
the keyboard, translated from scancodes into characters and keys
.TP
.IR eiafs (4)
the serial port, read and written from ring 3
.TP
.IR consrv (4)
the console, served a line at a time
.TP
.IR intuition (4)
the draw server, compositor and window manager
.PD
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR srv (3),
.IR init (8)
